1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Most AI agents browse the web by clicking and typing —

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
guessing their way through interfaces built for humans.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Slow, fragile, and hard to trust.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
WebMCP changes that: websites can now expose
structured tools an agent calls directly.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
This is PatchWork — a shared urban garden
where you and your agent plant together.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Here's the garden. Two beds, a 4-by-6 grid each.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
I plant tomatoes and basil — the rules engine
tells me they're good companions.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
But if I put potatoes next to tomatoes,
it warns me they're poor companions.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The same engine powers the diagnosis page:
yellow leaves plus brown spots

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
on a tomato gives Early Blight, with concrete actions.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Now the interesting part. I open PatchWork
in Chrome with WebMCP enabled.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
It discovers six tools — here they are.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Let me ask it to design a bed for me.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Watch: the agent calls design_bed,
the rules engine validates every placement,

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
and the canvas updates live — I can see exactly
what it did in the activity log.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Now let's push it.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The agent gets back a structured warning —
poor companions —

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
and explains it instead of silently breaking the garden.

19
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
It calls diagnose_issue and returns Early Blight
with actions,

20
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
using the same engine I use in the UI.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Why does WebMCP matter here? Because we share
the same state — same store, same canvas.

22
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The agent doesn't simulate my garden;
it works on the real one.

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Every action is validated and logged,
so trust is visible.

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:15,000
And planning becomes a negotiation: ask for
a spring plan with four hours of sun,

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
and it proposes crops that actually fit, with reasons.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,000
PatchWork is open source — the Learn page
documents all six tools,

27
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
and the repo is linked below.

28
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Open it in Chrome or ChatGPT
and grow something together.

29
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Thanks for watching!
